1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,120
One request to an AI agent can lead to many actions across connected systems.

2
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:12,240
Broad permissions can let a mistake go further than intended. Malicious instructions hidden in

3
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:18,360
retrieved content can redirect the agent. And when records are scattered, a reviewer must

4
00:00:18,360 --> 00:00:24,480
reconstruct what the agent requested, what was permitted, and what actually happened.

5
00:00:25,980 --> 00:00:31,832
Imagine you are investigating failed logins in a staging customer portal. A recent configuration

6
00:00:31,832 --> 00:00:37,685
change may be involved. You ask the agent to investigate. ScopedAct checks its requests

7
00:00:37,685 --> 00:00:43,538
against the task’s permissions and records the results. If the agent proposes a repair,

8
00:00:43,538 --> 00:00:49,390
you review the exact change before it can be applied.

9
00:00:50,890 --> 00:00:58,513
The evaluation offers two access profiles. Diagnostic access permits diagnostic reads. Change-proposal access also

10
00:00:58,513 --> 00:01:06,137
permits reading authentication configuration and proposing an update. Applying that update still requires approval.

11
00:01:06,137 --> 00:01:13,760
These profiles let you compare permission boundaries for the same incident.

12
00:01:15,260 --> 00:01:21,070
In this recorded evaluation, the agent read login events and deployment history. It then

13
00:01:21,070 --> 00:01:26,880
requested authentication configuration. That request was outside the task’s permissions. ScopedAct blocked it before

14
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:32,690
the protected connector executed it. The denial came from the actual request, not from

15
00:01:32,690 --> 00:01:38,500
a warning added to the demonstration.

16
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:46,950
After the denial, the guided agent paused. The operator could continue with permitted evidence

17
00:01:46,950 --> 00:01:53,900
or end the evaluation. Continuing did not add permissions. In this recorded run, the

18
00:01:53,900 --> 00:02:00,850
agent then finished without making another request. Ending the evaluation revoked its remaining access.

19
00:02:02,350 --> 00:02:07,997
The activity map connects the task, agent, and requested resources. It shows recorded attempts

20
00:02:07,997 --> 00:02:13,645
and their outcomes. Expand a resource to inspect the individual requests. This helps you

21
00:02:13,645 --> 00:02:19,292
review the activity for one assignment without piecing together unrelated records. The map covers

22
00:02:19,292 --> 00:02:24,940
requests through ScopedAct’s gateway.

23
00:02:26,440 --> 00:02:31,915
In another recorded run, the agent read four permitted resources and then stopped without

24
00:02:31,915 --> 00:02:37,390
proposing a repair. The incident was not confirmed fixed. This is an important distinction:

25
00:02:37,390 --> 00:02:42,865
permission to act does not mean the agent has found the right answer. The

26
00:02:42,865 --> 00:02:48,340
console keeps completed actions separate from confirmed recovery.

27
00:02:49,840 --> 00:02:55,302
Use the evaluation to inspect a blocked request, review any proposed change, and check

28
00:02:55,302 --> 00:03:00,765
the recorded outcome. The question is whether the agent’s actions stay within its authority,

29
00:03:00,765 --> 00:03:06,228
and whether you can understand and stop its activity. ScopedAct demonstrates these controls in

30
00:03:06,228 --> 00:03:11,690
a local test environment with synthetic data.
